# Copy to .env.local and fill in. .env.local is gitignored — never commit it.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL — the POSTGRES CONNECTION STRING.
#
# This is NOT the project URL (https://<ref>.supabase.co). That is the API
# endpoint and cannot be used to connect to the database.
#
# Supabase dashboard -> your project -> "Connect" (top bar)
#   -> Connection string -> Session pooler   <-- port 5432, this one
#
# Session pooler (5432), NOT transaction pooler (6543): these scripts run long
# transactions and prepared statements, which transaction mode does not
# support. The app itself will use 6543 later; the tooling uses 5432.
#
# Also not db.<ref>.supabase.co:5432 — the direct host is IPv6-only on the
# free tier and unreachable from most networks.
#
# Replace [YOUR-PASSWORD] with the database password. If you don't have it:
#   Project Settings -> Database -> Reset database password.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres.<PROJECT_REF>:<PASSWORD>@aws-0-<REGION>.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres

# Local Docker Postgres, for exercising the tooling without touching live data:
# docker run -d --name kora-pg -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=postgres -p 55432:5432 postgres:16
# MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:postgres@localhost:55432/postgres

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OPTION B — read-only access via PostgREST.
#
# Enough to run `pnpm migrate:preflight`, which is the item with the longest
# human lead time (the data-cleanup list is work someone has to do in the old
# app before cutover). NOT enough for the migration itself: PostgREST has no
# transactions, no TRUNCATE and no DDL, and the backfill is deliberately one
# all-or-nothing transaction.
#
# Both values already exist in the old app's Vercel project:
#   Vercel -> kora project -> Settings -> Environment Variables
# or Supabase -> Project Settings -> API.
#
# The service-role key bypasses row-level security entirely. Treat it as being
# exactly as sensitive as the database password.
#
# If MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL is also set, it takes precedence.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SUPABASE_URL=https://<PROJECT_REF>.supabase.co
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=<service role key>

# ===========================================================================
# THE APPLICATION (step 9+). Everything above is migration tooling only.
#
# DO NOT COPY THIS FILE VERBATIM INTO A HOSTING DASHBOARD. Three variables are
# deliberately different in a deployment, and getting them wrong produces a
# confusing outage rather than an error:
#
#   DATABASE_URL            local Postgres here; the Supabase TRANSACTION
#                           pooler (:6543) there. A deployed app pointed at
#                           localhost returns a generic 500 from every route
#                           and bounces SSO to "temporarily unavailable",
#                           neither of which mentions the database.
#   KORA_APP_URL            localhost here; the real origin there. SSO builds
#                           its redirect_uri from this, so a stale value sends
#                           Microsoft's authorization codes to a laptop.
#   MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL  NOT SET AT ALL in a deployment. It points at
#                           production and exists only for the migrate:* CLI.
#
# `GET /api/health` reports all three, and the app refuses to boot in
# production against a localhost database.
# ===========================================================================

# The TRANSACTION pooler — port 6543, not 5432. Same host and credentials as
# MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL above; only the port differs. The app opens many short
# connections and does not use prepared statements (`prepare: false`), which is
# exactly what transaction mode is for.
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres.<PROJECT_REF>:<PASSWORD>@aws-0-<REGION>.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres

# Storage: attachment signing, uploads, and the nightly backup.
# SUPABASE_URL=https://<PROJECT_REF>.supabase.co
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=<service role key>

# Signs session tokens and SSO state. MUST BE FRESH — not the old app's value.
# Reusing it would let tokens issued by the old app validate against this one.
#   openssl rand -base64 48
# INTEGTRACK_SECRET=

# The app's own absolute URL. NO TRAILING SLASH: the SSO redirect URI is built
# from this and Entra matches redirect URIs as exact strings, so a stray slash
# produces AADSTS50011 and sends you hunting for a credentials problem that
# does not exist.
# KORA_APP_URL=https://<project>.vercel.app

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Scheduled jobs. Vercel sends `Authorization: Bearer $CRON_SECRET` to cron
# endpoints once this is set on the project.
#
# MANDATORY. With it unset both cron endpoints return 503 rather than running
# unauthenticated — the old app wrapped its whole check in `if (CRON_SECRET)`,
# so a missing value silently removed authentication from an endpoint that
# dumps every user row.
#   openssl rand -base64 32
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CRON_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Microsoft Entra — ONE app registration serves both SSO and mail, through two
# different OAuth flows.
#
#   SSO   delegated authorization-code. Scopes openid/profile/email/User.Read,
#         all user-consentable.
#   Mail  client-credentials with the APPLICATION permission Mail.Send, which
#         requires admin consent from a tenant admin.
#
# Register two redirect URIs, platform "Web" (not SPA — a Web client sends the
# client secret, an SPA cannot):
#   http://localhost:3000/api/auth/microsoft/callback
#   https://<project>.vercel.app/api/auth/microsoft/callback
#
# Preview deployments get a fresh hostname each time, so SSO does not work on
# them. Username/password does.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AZURE_CLIENT_ID=
# AZURE_TENANT_ID=
# AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# The single mailbox all mail is sent AS — never the person who clicked send.
#
# App-only Mail.Send is tenant-wide by default: this registration can send as
# ANY mailbox. Ask an Exchange admin to scope it to just this one, via an
# Application Access Policy or Exchange RBAC for Applications.
# AZURE_DEFAULT_MAIL_SENDER=kora@kognozconsulting.com

# Optional second gate on SSO: the Entra account's email must sit at one of
# these domains. Comma-separated; a leading @ is tolerated. Unset means no
# restriction.
#
# Redundant on its own — the never-provision gate already requires a matching
# row in `users`. It guards the mistake ONE LEVEL UP: an admin adding a
# contractor or a client contact to the users table, who could then sign in
# with their own Microsoft account. Matched exactly, never as a suffix, so
# `notkognozconsulting.com` cannot slip through.
# AZURE_ALLOWED_DOMAIN=kognozconsulting.com

# Renders and logs mail instead of sending it. Lets the digest, the cron auth
# and the client-email path all be exercised end to end before the Azure app
# registration exists.
# KORA_MAIL_TRANSPORT=console

# ===========================================================================
# DEVELOPMENT TOOLING ONLY. Nothing below belongs in a deployment — every
# deployment-critical variable is above this line.
#
# Listed because this file should be a complete answer to "what does this app
# read", and an undocumented variable is indistinguishable from a typo.
# ===========================================================================

# Set by the framework, never by you. Read in lib/db/client.ts (the guard that
# refuses to boot production against a localhost database), lib/auth/cookies.ts
# (the `__Host-` cookie prefix requires Secure, so localhost drops it) and
# lib/auth/microsoft/state.ts. Setting it by hand in .env.local will produce
# confusing behaviour in exactly those three places.
# NODE_ENV=development

# The password `pnpm seed:auth-dev` gives every seeded local user. Local
# fixtures only — these accounts exist solely in your own Postgres.
# Unset means the script picks a random one and prints it.
# KORA_DEV_PASSWORD=

# Read by scripts/migrate/lib/rest.ts, the PostgREST fallback used when only
# read-only access is available (preflight without a database URL). Same value
# as SUPABASE_URL; separate name because it is the client-side convention.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://<PROJECT_REF>.supabase.co

# Read by scripts/set-db-url.mjs (`pnpm db:url`), which assembles a pooler
# connection string rather than making you hand-edit host, port and username.
# REGION defaults to aws-1-ap-south-1; REF has no default.
# SUPABASE_PROJECT_REF=
# SUPABASE_REGION=aws-1-ap-south-1
